<% content_for :title, "IFS & Addiction | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="ifs-addiction-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="ifs-addiction-hero">
    <picture class="ifs-addiction-background">
      <source media="(max-width: 767px)"
              srcset="<%= asset_path('addiction_ifs_mobile.png') %>">
      <%= image_tag "addiction_ifs_desktop.png",
          alt: "",
          class: "ifs-addiction-background-image",
          fetchpriority: "high" %>
    </picture>

    <div class="ifs-addiction-hero-content">
      <p class="ifs-addiction-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / ADDICTION / PERSPECTIVES / IFS
      </p>
      <h1>
        <span>ADDICTION</span>
        <span>THROUGH AN</span>
        <span>IFS LENS.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="ifs-addiction-intro">
        MEET YOUR PARTS.<br>
        GET CURIOUS ABOUT THE WHOLE STORY.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 01 / OPENING -->
  <section class="ia-section ia-pink">
    <div class="ia-heading">
      <p class="ia-number">01 / ANOTHER WAY TO LOOK</p>
      <h2>WHO'S<br>IN THE<br>ROOM?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ia-copy">
      <blockquote>
        "While all parts are welcome, all behavior is not."
        <cite>— IFS Institute, Community Guidelines</cite>
      </blockquote>
      <p class="ia-lead">
        You can understand why a part of you wanted something without approving of what you did to get it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Internal Family Systems (IFS) views the mind as a collection of parts with different fears, needs, and protective strategies. One part may want relief, another may seek control, and another may carry shame or rejection. Addiction can involve these parts pulling you in different directions. Instead of dismissing them as irrational, IFS asks what each part was trying to accomplish—and whether its strategy caused harm.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 / PARTS -->
  <section class="ia-section ia-yellow">
    <div class="ia-heading">
      <p class="ia-number">02 / DIFFERENT PARTS, DIFFERENT CONCERNS</p>
      <h2>MAKE ROOM<br>TO<br>LISTEN.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ia-copy">
      <p class="ia-lead">
        Different parts can want completely different things—and each can feel like the whole truth.
      </p>
      <p>
        In your First DUI, part of you wanted freedom from being bedridden, while another desperately wanted your physical pain to stop. In your Third DUI, one part recognized danger while another wanted alcohol immediately. In Press Send, a vulnerable part feared rejection while another attempted to control the situation through angry messages. These are ways to explore inner conflict, not fixed labels you have to force onto every feeling.
      </p>
      <div class="ia-grid">
        <div class="ia-box">
          <h3>THE PART SEEKING RELIEF</h3>
          <p>Wants pain, restlessness, or emotional distress to stop now, even when the chosen solution has consequences.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ia-box">
          <h3>THE PART WANTING CONTROL</h3>
          <p>Tries to keep things manageable, anticipate trouble, or regain control when life feels unpredictable.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ia-box">
          <h3>THE PART CARRYING SHAME</h3>
          <p>Holds painful feelings of rejection, helplessness, or regret that other parts may try to keep out of view.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ia-box">
          <h3>THE PART WANTING FREEDOM</h3>
          <p>Wants independence, movement, connection, and a life that feels larger than its restrictions.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Understanding these competing needs can help you see why the urge to drink felt compelling without making the resulting choices harmless.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 / PROTECTION -->
  <section class="ia-section ia-purple">
    <div class="ia-heading">
      <p class="ia-number">03 / PROTECTIVE INTENTIONS</p>
      <h2>WHAT WAS<br>IT TRYING<br>TO DO?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ia-copy">
      <p class="ia-lead">
        IFS distinguishes between a part's protective intention and the consequences of its behavior.
      </p>
      <p>
        Managers attempt to prevent distress through planning, control, or criticism. Firefighters react when distress is already overwhelming, seeking immediate relief. Exiles carry painful feelings such as helplessness, abandonment, and shame. Alcohol can become a firefighter's quick solution, while a manager may try to contain the damage or keep appearances intact. Neither role automatically explains every drinking decision.
      </p>
      <div class="ia-grid">
        <div class="ia-box">
          <h3>MANAGERS</h3>
          <p>Try to prevent pain and regain control. In Press Send, your controlling response may have tried to force reassurance or a response.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ia-box">
          <h3>FIREFIGHTERS</h3>
          <p>Seek relief right now. In your First DUI, alcohol promised an escape from pain; in your Third DUI, <em>Jackpot</em> seized an opportunity to drink.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        A part may believe it is protecting you while using a strategy that endangers you or harms others. Curiosity about that intention is a beginning, not an excuse.
      </p>
      <div class="ia-callout">
        UNDERSTAND THE PART.<br>
        DON'T EXCUSE THE ACTION.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 / CURIOSITY -->
  <section class="ia-section ia-black">
    <div class="ia-heading">
      <p class="ia-number">04 / CURIOSITY & UNDERSTANDING</p>
      <h2>ASK<br>A BETTER<br>QUESTION.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ia-copy">
      <p class="ia-lead">
        Instead of stopping at "Why the fuck did I do that?", ask what was happening inside you.
      </p>
      <p>
        In your First DUI, a part wanted relief from severe pain and another wanted freedom from being stuck in bed. In your Third DUI, the firefighter's desire to drink argued with the manager's <em>No</em>. In Press Send, insecurity and fear of rejection may have been beneath the attempt to control the conversation. Identifying these needs doesn't change what happened, but it can clarify what needs attention before the next crisis.
      </p>
      <div class="ia-stack">
        <div class="ia-stack-item">
          <span>01</span>
          <div><h3>WHAT IS THIS PART CONCERNED ABOUT?</h3><p>Look for pain, abandonment, loss of freedom, fear, or consequences.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="ia-stack-item">
          <span>02</span>
          <div><h3>WHAT IS IT TRYING TO ACCOMPLISH?</h3><p>Relief, control, reassurance, safety, or connection?</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="ia-stack-item">
          <span>03</span>
          <div><h3>WHAT DOES IT WANT YOU TO UNDERSTAND?</h3><p>Notice the need without assuming its preferred action is the right one.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="ia-stack-item">
          <span>04</span>
          <div><h3>WHAT SUPPORT WOULD HELP?</h3><p>Consider appropriate treatment, trusted people, or an earlier plan for high-risk moments.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 05 / UNBLENDING -->
  <section class="ia-section ia-pink">
    <div class="ia-heading">
      <p class="ia-number">05 / UNBLENDING & PERSPECTIVE</p>
      <h2>GIVE IT<br>SOME<br>SPACE.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ia-copy">
      <p class="ia-lead">
        A part can become so dominant that its urgency feels like your whole identity.
      </p>
      <p>
        IFS calls this <em>blending</em>. In your Third DUI, the firefighter's <em>Jackpot</em> overwhelmed the manager's <em>No</em>, while mania further compromised judgment. Unblending means noticing a part's perspective without automatically following it. It can be easier to practice while stable; IFS alone cannot explain or manage the biological effects of severe mania or intoxication.
      </p>
      <div class="ia-grid">
        <div class="ia-box">
          <h3>THE WHOLE VERDICT</h3>
          <p>"I have to drink. Nothing else matters."</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ia-box">
          <h3>A PART'S EXPERIENCE</h3>
          <p>"A part of me urgently wants alcohol. Another part recognizes the danger."</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        The shift doesn't make an urge disappear. It makes room to recognize conflicting needs and to seek support before the impulse becomes overwhelming.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 06 / COMPASSION AND ACCOUNTABILITY -->
  <section class="ia-section ia-yellow">
    <div class="ia-heading">
      <p class="ia-number">06 / COMPASSION & ACCOUNTABILITY</p>
      <h2>UNDERSTAND.<br>TAKE<br>RESPONSIBILITY.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ia-copy">
      <p class="ia-lead">
        Protective intentions don't cancel harmful consequences.
      </p>
      <p>
        In your First DUI, a part sought relief and control, but you still made the decision to drink and drive. In your Third DUI, mania affected your judgment, but the consequences of your actions remained real. In Press Send, a part tried to regain control by making someone understand your pain, but the messages could hurt people you cared about. IFS lets you examine those motivations without handing responsibility to a part as though it were somebody else.
      </p>
      <div class="ia-grid">
        <div class="ia-box">
          <h3>THE INTENTION</h3>
          <p>Relieve pain, stop distress, regain control, or obtain reassurance.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ia-box">
          <h3>THE EFFECT</h3>
          <p>Dangerous decisions, damaged trust, and consequences that outlast the moment.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ia-box">
          <h3>THE NEED</h3>
          <p>Real relief, stability, freedom, and connection still deserve attention.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ia-box">
          <h3>THE RESPONSE</h3>
          <p>Accept responsibility, make amends where appropriate, respect boundaries, and plan safer responses.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Compassion can help you investigate why a behavior happened. Accountability means dealing with what it did.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 07 / SELF AND SUPPORT -->
  <section class="ia-section ia-purple">
    <div class="ia-heading">
      <p class="ia-number">07 / SELF & SUPPORT</p>
      <h2>LEAD<br>WITH<br>CURIOSITY.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ia-copy">
      <p class="ia-lead">
        Self-leadership means responding with clarity and care instead of letting one part make every decision.
      </p>
      <p>
        IFS describes Self through qualities such as curiosity, compassion, calm, and confidence. For you, that might mean recognizing when a firefighter wants alcohol, when a manager is trying to control a relationship, or when an exile feels trapped or rejected. The point isn't to silence these parts. It's to understand their needs while finding responses that don't create further harm.
      </p>
      <div class="ia-grid">
        <div class="ia-box">
          <h3>CURIOSITY</h3>
          <p>Ask what a part fears and what it believes its behavior will accomplish.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ia-box">
          <h3>COMPASSION</h3>
          <p>Recognize genuine suffering without approving of harmful strategies.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ia-box">
          <h3>CLARITY</h3>
          <p>Consider all the competing needs—and the consequences of acting on one of them.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ia-box">
          <h3>SUPPORT</h3>
          <p>Use treatment, trusted people, and practical plans when reflection alone isn't enough.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Across First DUI, Third DUI, and Press Send, the recurring conflict is between seeking immediate relief or control and protecting your longer-term well-being and relationships. Understanding your parts can make that conflict clearer; it doesn't erase your choices or their consequences.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="ifs-addiction-ending">
    <div class="ia-ending-heading">
      <p class="ia-ending-small">DIFFERENT PARTS. YOUR WHOLE LIFE.</p>
      <h2>MAKE<br>ROOM TO<br><span>UNDERSTAND.</span></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ia-ending-copy">
      <p>
        You can listen to the parts that seek relief, control, and connection without allowing their strategies to determine what you do. All parts may be welcome, but harmful actions still require accountability. Understanding the need is how you begin to find a different response.
      </p>
      <%= link_to "← BACK TO ADDICTION PERSPECTIVES",
          addiction_perspectives_path,
          class: "ia-back-button" %>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>
